perations, program planning, practices, policies, and procedures. Promotes and supports compliance with legislation on accessibility, anti-racism, diversity, inclusion, equity and human rights, and supports the university through employment systems reviews and employment equity audits. Continues the development of EDI and anti-racism training, resources, and supports to TRU community members. Coordinates the establishment of a pan-institutional EDI Advisory Group to provide feedback on all EDI and anti-racism efforts. Works closely with Indigenous Education staff, Elders, and Secwépemc Cultural Advisor to support cultural competence of staff and students, as well as community relations. Coordinates the development of initiatives aimed at increasing visibility for equity-deserving students, faculty, and staff and enhancing a campus culture of EDI. Leads collaborative, cooperative, and consultative efforts to create a common purpose and foster the spirit of teamwork that is inclusive of the university community. Leads planning processes, manages resources and provides creative leadership to faculty and staff in an academic setting. Develops and promotes TRU’s mission, vision, and values through a lens of intersectionality and belonging. Builds and maintains a cohesive and highly functioning team within the Office of EDI & AR. Stays current on best practices in post-secondary EDI & AR work. Staff Relations Plans and prioritizes human resource needs for the Office of EDI & AR, ensuring that viable departmental hiring plans are in place. Supervises staff including goal setting, performance evaluation, coaching and supporting professional development. Works to build and maintain trust between administration, faculty, staff and other stakeholders. Encourages a sense of vision, energy and collegiality within the Office of EDI & AR. Integrates Equity, Diversity, Inclusion and Anti-Racism principles in all recruitment activities. Works to promote retention of staff. General Management Develops and tracks performance indicators and metrics for the Office of EDI & AR and provides the Provost with an annual performance analysis. Ensures the effective management of a balanced operating budget. Develops, implements, monitors, and reviews tactical and operational plans, procedures, systems and supports in accordance with the university’s strategic goals, policies, procedures, and regulatory requirements. Relationships Within and Outside the University Builds effective relationships with a broad spectrum of constituents, including senior administration, faculty members, students, alumni, community leaders, Indigenous communities, industry and professional leaders, bargaining units, educational and other key institutions regionally, provincially, nationally, internationally and with organizations in the academic academy. Promotes and advocates for the Office of EDI & AR, raising awareness of its contributions. Represents TRU and its interests in provincial and national organizations that span the post-secondary education sector. Plays a key role in encouraging exchanges and collaborative initiatives with other institutions, business communities and professions locally, nationally and internationally. Serves as the chief spokesperson for the Office of Equity, Diversity, Inclusion and Anti-Racism across TRU and in the external community, while seeking guidance and support from the Office of University Relations and Marketing and Communications for all media enquiries. REPORTS TO Provost and Vice-President Academic QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ATTRIBUTES A candidate must hold a Master’s degree and have significant leadership experience that is particularly relevant to EDI and anti-racism, ideally in a post-secondary institution. Preference will be given to an equity deserving candidate with lived experience and a demonstrated understanding of EDI and anti-racism concepts and frameworks 5-7 years of related experience should include: An