    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/34935": {
      "title": "Director, Integrated Planning & Effectiveness - (01248.5)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details Director, Integrated Planning & Effectiveness - (01248.5) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 8/7/2026 Closing Application Date 28/8/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 14/9/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES Under the direction of the AVP, Integrated Planning & Effectiveness, the Director, provides strategic and operational leadership to the Integrated Planning & Effectiveness (IPE) team and manages the day-to-day activities in institutional research, planning and analytics. The Director, IPE provides leadership and expertise to the university’s planning, analysis, and institutional research functions. The Director is responsible for overseeing the collection, analysis, interpretation, and dissemination of information and ensures business analytics products are developed and distributed appropriately to support strategic goals and priorities for evidence-based planning, decision-making and monitoring institutional performance. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES The Director, Integrated Planning and Effectiveness (IPE) manages, directs and ensures the effective day to day operation of the IPE department services for a wide scope of accountability and operational projects. Under the general supervision of the Associate Vice-President IPE, the incumbent provides leadership and expertise to the university planning, analysis, and assessment functions. As an operational leader, the Director sees the larger picture of how IPE serves the university decision making. This requires an excellent understanding of most university areas, including business and academic functions.  An effective Director has the ability to see what is required to better serve client needs. As well, the Director requires an ability to communicate and form trusting relationships with university and external colleagues. The Director needs to be scanning the environment for information and techniques to improve processes and ensure the information is relevant to audiences. The Director serves in an advisory and decision support role to the University senior academic and administrative executive and takes on additional responsibilities and duties on behalf of the AVP as needed. Specific Accountabilities: Manage, direct, and ensure the effective day to day operation of the Integrated Planning and Effectiveness department. Through the selection, orientation, motivation, and development of IPE staff. Provide leadership of IPE and TRU branding and voice in the work of the department. Assists the AVP in the development of budgets and long-range plans for the office and identifying resource requirements to ensure the effectiveness of the office. Develops data quality standards and process and ensure the standards are met. Fosters an environment of collaboration and a culture of ongoing service excellence within IPE.  Inspires, motivates, and guides team members by fostering commitment, team spirit and trust. Supports the AVP in policy development and interpretation. Development of a strategic institutional research program to support the analytical, planning, and assessment functions of the university. Ensure a comprehensive and ongoing program of institutional data collection, analysis, and dissemination in support of management, strategic planning and decision-making at all levels of university operations, particularly those related to enrollment management, student outcomes, external rankings, and institutional effectiveness. Oversee the design and performs complex analytical studies and projects pertinent to aspects of the operation and strategic direction of the University. Manages the collection and organization of data and information for such studies; and ensures these results are shared with relevant stakeholders. Provide standard management information and official statistics for both internal and external reporting. Oversee provincial and federal accountability reporting for TRU including annual Accountability Plans and Reports, Audited Full-Time Equivalent enrolment reports, Central Data Warehouse submissions, Annual Performance Measures, and Statistics Canada data submissions and ensures that timelines are met. Oversee institutional participation in provincial, national, and international research benchmarking initiatives. Collaborates with other divisional leaders including ITS and RO offices and with internal customers to ensure that business intelligence products and related processes and systems are optimized to foster data quality, data integrity, and delivery of effective reporting and data/information solutions. Envisions, coordinates and ensures the development and maintenance of decision-support systems and tools and provides training and assistance in their use. Translate data into usable information for planning, assessment, and other decision-making purposes. Lead and manage contracts with external agencies for specialized planning and research projects. Foments and advances a culture of integrated planning at TRU. Provide support for the design, development, and implementation of integrated planning processes, and providing support for budget planning and resource allocation. Support institutional and divisional strategic planning initiatives through a variety of methods considering innovation and futureproofing. Continuously develops skills and knowledge in professional practice. Maintain currency in best practices regarding research in higher education and in the field of Institutional Research & Planning. Represent TRU on provincial, national, and international committees and initiatives. Establishes a high personal standard of service and performance by assuming additional responsibilities and duties as assigned by the AVP. Facilitates, chairs, and participates in assigned teams and committees as required. REPORTS TO Associate Vice-President, Integrated Planning and Effectiveness QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS High degree of expertise with information technology as well as demonstrated competence with data analytics, planning, assessment, performance measurement and research methods. Excellent organization, presentation, written and oral communication and facilitation skills. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with staff, faculty, and administrators at all levels. Plan, develop, organize, and implement short- and long-term projects and strategies within an environment of frequently changing priorities. Demonstrated team building skills and have a client service orientation. Understanding of current and emergent research and higher education trends Other relevant skills such as LEAN training and project management are considered an advantage. Possess sound skills in dealing with controversial and sensitive issues, which involve balancing interests of numerous parties with divergent viewpoints. Exercise sound judgement and political acumen Education and experience: Master’s degree in a relevant area. Ten years of progressively responsible experience in research and planning, including a minimum of five years management experience Experience working in a post-secondary institution is preferred. Pay Band $139,899.00 - $164,592.00 ( Admin Pay Band DH ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Integrated Planning and Effectiveness Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.3 Last sync: 2026-09-03 22:55:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 8/7/2026 Closing Application Date 28/8/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 14/9/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES Under the direction of the AVP, Integrated Planning & Effectiveness, the Director, provides strategic and operational leadership to the Integrated Planning & Effectiveness (IPE) team and manages the day-to-day activities in institutional research, planning and analytics. The Director, IPE provides leadership and expertise to the university’s planning, analysis, and institutional research functions. The Director is responsible for overseeing the collection, analysis, interpretation, and dissemination of information and ensures business analytics products are developed and distributed appropriately to support strategic goals and priorities for evidence-based planning, decision-making and monitoring institutional performance. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES The Director, Integrated Planning and Effectiveness (IPE) manages, directs and ensures the effective day to day operation of the IPE department services for a wide scope of accountability and operational projects. Under the general supervision of the Associate Vice-President IPE, the incumbent provides leadership and expertise to the university planning, analysis, and assessment functions. As an operational leader, the Director sees the larger picture of how IPE serves the university decision making. This requires an excellent understanding of most university areas, including business and academic functions.  An effective Director has the ability to see what is required to better serve client needs. As well, the Director requires an ability to communicate and form trusting relationships with university and external colleagues. The Director needs to be scanning the environment for information and techniques to improve processes and ensure the information is relevant to audiences. The Director serves in an advisory and decision support role to the University senior academic and administrative executive and takes on additional responsibilities and duties on behalf of the AVP as needed. Specific Accountabilities: Manage, direct, and ensure the effective day to day operation of the Integrated Planning and Effectiveness department. Through the selection, orientation, motivation, and development of IPE staff. Provide leadership of IPE and TRU branding and voice in the work of the department. Assists the AVP in the development of budgets and long-range plans for the office and identifying resource requirements to ensure the effectiveness of the office. Develops data quality standards and process and ensure the standards are met. Fosters an environment of collaboration and a culture of ongoing service excellence within IPE.  Inspires, motivates, and guides team members by fostering commitment, team spirit and trust. Supports the AVP in policy development and interpretation. Development of a strategic institutional research program to support the analytical, planning, and assessment functions of the university. Ensure a comprehensive and ongoing program of institutional data collection, analysis, and dissemination in support of management, strategic planning and decision-making at all levels of university operations, particularly those related to enrollment management, student outcomes, external rankings, and institutional effectiveness. Oversee the design and performs complex analytical studies and projects pertinent to aspects of the operation and strategic direction of the University. Manages the collection and organization of data and information for such studies; and ensures these results are shared with relevant stakeholders. Provide standard management information and official statistics for both internal and external reporting. Oversee provincial and federal accountability reporting for TRU including annual Accountability Plans and Reports, Audited Full-Time Equivalent enrolment reports, Central Data Warehouse submissions, Annual Performance Measures, and Statistics Canada data submissions and ensures that timelines are met. Oversee institutional participation in provincial, national, and international research benchmarking initiatives. Collaborates with other divisional leaders including ITS and RO offices and with internal customers to ensure that business intelligence products and related processes and systems are optimized to foster data quality, data integrity, and delivery of effective reporting and data/information solutions. Envisions, coordinates and ensures the development and maintenance of decision-support systems and tools and provides training and assistance in their use. Translate data into usable information for planning, assessment, and other decision-making purposes. Lead and manage contracts with external agencies for specialized planning and research projects. Foments and advances a culture of integrated planning at TRU. Provide support for the design, development, and implementation of integrated planning processes, and providing support for budget planning and resource allocation. Support institutional and divisional strategic planning initiatives through a variety of methods considering innovation and futureproofing. Continuously develops skills and knowledge in professional practice. Maintain currency in best practices regarding research in higher education and in the field of Institutional Research & Planning. Represent TRU on provincial, national, and international committees and initiatives. Establishes a high personal standard of service and performance by assuming additional responsibilities and duties as assigned by the AVP. Facilitates, chairs, and participates in assigned teams and committees as required. REPORTS TO Associate Vice-President, Integrated Planning and Effectiveness QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS High degree of expertise with information technology as well as demonstrated competence with data analytics, planning, assessment, performance measurement and research methods. Excellent organization, presentation, written and oral communication and facilitation skills. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with staff, faculty, and administrators at all levels. Plan, develop, organize, and implement short- and long-term projects and strategies within an environment of frequently changing priorities. Demonstrated team building skills and have a client service orientation. Understanding of current and emergent research and higher education trends Other relevant skills such as LEAN training and project management are considered an advantage. Possess sound skills in dealing with controversial and sensitive issues, which involve balancing interests of numerous parties with divergent viewpoints. Exercise sound judgement and political acumen Education and experience: Master’s degree in a relevant area. Ten years of progressively responsible experience in research and planning, including a minimum of five years management experience Experience working in a post-secondary institution is preferred. Pay Band $139,899.00 - $164,592.00 ( Admin Pay Band DH ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Integrated Planning and Effectiveness Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
    },
    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/34884": {
      "title": "Sessional - Faculty - Fall 2026 - (02050.11811)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details Sessional - Faculty - Fall 2026 - (02050.11811) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Faculty (Sessional) Posting In effect from 3/7/2026 Closing Application Date 17/7/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Instructional Type Academic/Career(max 24 credits) Anticipated Start Date 9/9/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) 23/12/2026 DUTIES The School of Nursing, BScN program, is seeking sessional faculty for the Fall 2025 semester. Courses are scheduled from September 9 to December 11, 2026, with an additional week for practice course evaluations and an exam period for theory courses. Positions are available in: (YEAR 1) NURS1700 - Professionalism and Leadership 1: Introduction to the Profession of Nursing - This course is an introduction to the profession of nursing. Students are introduced to the curriculum's foundational perspectives and concepts and how these relate to nursing practice. Students explore nursing history, the evolution of nursing, and the health care system. Students will explore their individual and professional values and beliefs to develop their professional nursing self-concept. Students critically reflect on the role of gender, race, and class in the social construction of nursing as a profession, explore their responsibility for safe and ethical nursing practice, and discover the governing bodies that regulate nursing. (YEAR 2) NURS2740 - Nursing Practice 3: Promoting Health and Healing - In this nursing practice experience, students will develop caring relationships with individuals and families to promote health and support understanding of their health and healing processes when experiencing more complex health challenges, both episodic and chronic. Students will have opportunities to practice nursing approaches that accompany this understanding. Students will work with families and individuals in the home and community, in agencies, and in care facilities to incorporate concepts and learning from all the courses in this semester into their nursing practice. The community and society are considered as contextual influences on the promotion of health for the individual and the family. Areas of Practice include Mental Health, Community, med/surg, and Maternity. (YEAR 3) HLSC3650 - Health Sciences: Pathophysiology - This course builds on concepts introduced in Pathophysiology 1 with an emphasis on complex health alterations. Students will learn about the presentation and pathogenesis of health alterations across the life span. In selected units, diagnostics, epidemiology, epigenetics, nutrition, and environmental impacts will be introduced. Topics will be closely aligned with current nursing practice and connected to concepts in other nursing courses. (YEAR 3) NURS3740 - Nursing Practice 5: Promoting Health and Healing - This nursing practice experience continues to provide opportunities for students to develop caring relationships with individuals and families experiencing complex health challenges while coming to understand their health and healing processes. Students will have opportunities to practice nursing approaches that accompany this understanding. Students work with families and individuals in the home and community, in agencies, and in care facilities to incorporate concepts and learning from all the courses in this semester into their nursing practice. Areas of Practice include Acute med/surg. (YEAR 4) NURS4380 - Nursing Practice 7:Promoting Health and Healing in Complexity - This nursing practice experience continues to provide opportunities for students to develop caring relationships with individuals and families experiencing complex health challenges while coming to understand their health and healing processes. Students will have opportunities to practice nursing approaches that accompany this understanding. Students work with families and individuals in the home and community, in agencies, and in care facilities to incorporate concepts and learning from all the courses in this semester into their nursing practice. (YEAR 4) HLSC4650 - Health Sciences: Pathophysiology 3 - This course builds on the concepts introduced in Pathophysiology 1 with an emphasis on complex health alterations. Students will learn about the presentation and pathogenesis of health alterations across the lifespan. In selected units, diagnostics, epidemiology, epigenetics, nutrition, and environmental impacts will be explored. Topics will be closely aligned with current nursing practice and connected to concepts in other nursing courses. QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree required; Holds a Bachelors Degree in Nursing; Three (3) years full time or equivalent hours of nursing experience; Experience in teaching nursing and/or clinical practice; Evidence of continuous learning in your area of nursing; An understanding of, and experience in, the Canadian healthcare system; and Current BCCNM registration in good standing without limits or conditions, or eligibility for registration with BCCNM prior to appointment. *Official transcripts will be required prior to appointment. Pay Band $2,732.34 - $2,732.34 ( Sessional Faculty - Academic (per credit) ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 4 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> School of Nursing -> Bachelor of Nursing Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.3 Last sync: 2026-09-03 22:55:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Faculty (Sessional) Posting In effect from 3/7/2026 Closing Application Date 17/7/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Instructional Type Academic/Career(max 24 credits) Anticipated Start Date 9/9/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) 23/12/2026 DUTIES The School of Nursing, BScN program, is seeking sessional faculty for the Fall 2025 semester. Courses are scheduled from September 9 to December 11, 2026, with an additional week for practice course evaluations and an exam period for theory courses. Positions are available in: (YEAR 1) NURS1700 - Professionalism and Leadership 1: Introduction to the Profession of Nursing - This course is an introduction to the profession of nursing. Students are introduced to the curriculum's foundational perspectives and concepts and how these relate to nursing practice. Students explore nursing history, the evolution of nursing, and the health care system. Students will explore their individual and professional values and beliefs to develop their professional nursing self-concept. Students critically reflect on the role of gender, race, and class in the social construction of nursing as a profession, explore their responsibility for safe and ethical nursing practice, and discover the governing bodies that regulate nursing. (YEAR 2) NURS2740 - Nursing Practice 3: Promoting Health and Healing - In this nursing practice experience, students will develop caring relationships with individuals and families to promote health and support understanding of their health and healing processes when experiencing more complex health challenges, both episodic and chronic. Students will have opportunities to practice nursing approaches that accompany this understanding. Students will work with families and individuals in the home and community, in agencies, and in care facilities to incorporate concepts and learning from all the courses in this semester into their nursing practice. The community and society are considered as contextual influences on the promotion of health for the individual and the family. Areas of Practice include Mental Health, Community, med/surg, and Maternity. (YEAR 3) HLSC3650 - Health Sciences: Pathophysiology - This course builds on concepts introduced in Pathophysiology 1 with an emphasis on complex health alterations. Students will learn about the presentation and pathogenesis of health alterations across the life span. In selected units, diagnostics, epidemiology, epigenetics, nutrition, and environmental impacts will be introduced. Topics will be closely aligned with current nursing practice and connected to concepts in other nursing courses. (YEAR 3) NURS3740 - Nursing Practice 5: Promoting Health and Healing - This nursing practice experience continues to provide opportunities for students to develop caring relationships with individuals and families experiencing complex health challenges while coming to understand their health and healing processes. Students will have opportunities to practice nursing approaches that accompany this understanding. Students work with families and individuals in the home and community, in agencies, and in care facilities to incorporate concepts and learning from all the courses in this semester into their nursing practice. Areas of Practice include Acute med/surg. (YEAR 4) NURS4380 - Nursing Practice 7:Promoting Health and Healing in Complexity - This nursing practice experience continues to provide opportunities for students to develop caring relationships with individuals and families experiencing complex health challenges while coming to understand their health and healing processes. Students will have opportunities to practice nursing approaches that accompany this understanding. Students work with families and individuals in the home and community, in agencies, and in care facilities to incorporate concepts and learning from all the courses in this semester into their nursing practice. (YEAR 4) HLSC4650 - Health Sciences: Pathophysiology 3 - This course builds on the concepts introduced in Pathophysiology 1 with an emphasis on complex health alterations. Students will learn about the presentation and pathogenesis of health alterations across the lifespan. In selected units, diagnostics, epidemiology, epigenetics, nutrition, and environmental impacts will be explored. Topics will be closely aligned with current nursing practice and connected to concepts in other nursing courses. QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree required; Holds a Bachelors Degree in Nursing; Three (3) years full time or equivalent hours of nursing experience; Experience in teaching nursing and/or clinical practice; Evidence of continuous learning in your area of nursing; An understanding of, and experience in, the Canadian healthcare system; and Current BCCNM registration in good standing without limits or conditions, or eligibility for registration with BCCNM prior to appointment. *Official transcripts will be required prior to appointment. Pay Band $2,732.34 - $2,732.34 ( Sessional Faculty - Academic (per credit) ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 4 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> School of Nursing -> Bachelor of Nursing Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
